WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.439
Technical OSINT is the analysis of lawfully accessible public information about internet-facing infrastructure.

00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:15.987
Its sources can include domains, DNS records, internet addresses, certificates, websites, public repositories, package registries, cloud references, and technical metadata.

00:00:15.987 --> 00:00:19.762
The work is analytical rather than intrusive.

00:00:19.762 --> 00:00:27.090
Analysts collect observable records, preserve dates and source context, compare indicators, and develop cautious judgments.

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:35.305
Technical OSINT does not authorize scanning beyond approved limits, attempting access, using exposed credentials, or bypassing controls.

00:00:35.305 --> 00:00:47.074
Defenders use technical OSINT to improve attack-surface awareness, identify unknown or stale assets, support threat intelligence, assess vendor dependencies, and add public context during incidents.

00:00:47.074 --> 00:00:51.737
It can reveal questions that internal inventories should answer.

00:00:51.737 --> 00:00:55.512
Public records rarely tell the whole story.

00:00:55.512 --> 00:00:59.731
A domain may be parked, delegated, or abandoned.

00:00:59.731 --> 00:01:05.282
An address may belong to a cloud provider or shared host.

00:01:05.282 --> 00:01:13.942
A certificate may show that a name was validated at one time without proving current ownership or security.

00:01:13.942 --> 00:01:17.272
Strong analysis separates observation from inference.

00:01:17.272 --> 00:01:25.044
For example, a certificate record can support that a certificate covered a hostname on a date.

00:01:25.044 --> 00:01:32.372
Connecting that hostname to an organization or active system requires additional evidence and confidence language.

00:01:32.372 --> 00:01:36.590
The goal is responsible visibility and decision support.

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:49.247
Work from an authorized scope, minimize unnecessary collection, document every important source, validate across independent evidence, and report risk signals as signals rather than claims of compromise.
